# TASK: N1 ↔ C1 Codebase Hardgate Integration (Feature-Switched)

## ROLE

You are a senior integration engineer performing a surgical, zero-regression integration
of C1’s codebase-scan hardgate logic into the N1 codebase, controlled by a
feature flag. You will touch NOTHING outside the codebase hardgate execution path.

## REPOSITORIES

- N1 repo (primary target): [N1_REPO_PATH]
- C1 repo (logic source): [C1_REPO_PATH]

-----

## PHASE 0 — UNDERSTAND BEFORE TOUCHING ANYTHING

### 0.1 — Map N1 Hardgates

Search the N1 codebase and produce a complete inventory:

- Find all hardgate definitions and their categories
- Identify which are codebase-related (naming conventions, code quality,
  dependency checks, static analysis gates)
- Identify which are platform-related, DB-related, Kafka-related — mark these OFF-LIMITS
- Output: | HardgateName | Category | FilePath | MethodName | Line# |

### 0.2 — Map C1 Codebase Scan Logic

Search the C1 codebase and identify:

- Entry point(s) for codebase scanning / hardgate evaluation
- Interface/contract it exposes (input params, return types)
- Any dependencies it requires (clients, config, models)
- Output: EntryClass, Method signatures, Input/Output contracts

### 0.3 — Gap Analysis

Before writing a single line of code:

- Compare N1 codebase hardgate inputs vs C1 expected inputs
- Flag any mismatches in data shape, types, or missing context
- Confirm C1’s return type is compatible with what N1 expects downstream
- List every assumption. Stop and ask if any assumption is non-trivial.

-----

## PHASE 1 — FEATURE FLAG INFRASTRUCTURE

### 1.1 — Create Feature Flag

- Flag name: C1_CODEBASE_HARDGATE_ENABLED (boolean)
- Source from N1’s existing config mechanism — do NOT introduce a new config system
- Default: false (legacy behavior is the safe default)
- Add comment: purpose, owner, rollback instructions

### 1.2 — Validate Flag Plumbing

- Confirm the flag is readable at the codebase hardgate execution point only
- Do NOT propagate this flag to any class outside that path

-----

## PHASE 2 — C1 ADAPTER (NEW CODE ONLY)

### 2.1 — Create Adapter Interface

In the N1 codebase, create:

```java
CodebaseHardgateEvaluator {
    HardgateResult evaluate(CodebaseHardgateContext context);
}
```

- CodebaseHardgateContext contains exactly what N1 already has at the call site
- HardgateResult must match what N1’s downstream consumers already expect
- Do not invent new data fetching

### 2.2 — Implement Two Concrete Classes

1. N1LegacyCodebaseHardgateEvaluator — extract existing N1 logic into this,
   do not rewrite it
1. C1CodebaseHardgateEvaluator — calls C1 logic, adapts
   input/output inside this class only

### 2.3 — Wire the Switch

At the N1 hardgate execution point, replace existing call with:

```java
if (featureFlag.isEnabled("C1_CODEBASE_HARDGATE_ENABLED")) {
    return c1Evaluator.evaluate(context);
} else {
    return n1LegacyEvaluator.evaluate(context);
}
```

This is the ONLY change to existing N1 flow logic.

